1. Frontend Development

Frontend development deals with the visible part of a website or web application. It is the part that users can see and interact with.

Frontend developers use technologies like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Frameworks such as React can also be used.

Example: On an online shopping website like Amazon, the product images, search bar, buttons, menus, product details, and shopping cart interface are part of the frontend.

2. Backend Development

Backend development deals with the server-side part of a website. It works behind the scenes and handles things like business logic, user authentication, database operations, and processing requests.

Backend developers can use technologies such as Java, Python, Node.js, PHP, and databases like MySQL.

Example: When a user logs into Amazon, the backend checks the username and password and retrieves the user's account information from the database.

 3. Full-Stack Development

Full-stack development means working on both frontend and backend of a web application. A full-stack developer can develop the user interface as well as the server-side logic and database connection.

Example: In an online shopping application, a full-stack developer can create the product page using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript and also develop the backend that manages users, products, orders, and databases.

 Conclusion

In simple words, frontend is what the user sees, backend is what works behind the scenes, and full-stack development combines both frontend and backend development.
